WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.120
Robert Pattinson's performance in "The Batman"&nbsp;
is a fascinating look at the darkness that lurks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.560
inside the superhero. But there is one moment&nbsp;
that exposes the pain that Bruce Wayne suffers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.000
Here is the Bruce Wayne scene in&nbsp;
"The Batman" that went too far.&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.080
Fans of Batman are well aware that Bruce&nbsp;
Wayne has a lot of trauma to deal with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.200
especially when it comes to his family. As a young&nbsp;
boy, his parents are both murdered, leaving him an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.240
orphan. The tragic backstory is just a part of&nbsp;
what makes the character so rich and well-liked.&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.280
In Matt Reeves' "The Batman," this aspect of&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne is still a crucial part of his story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.120
Because of what Bruce goes through growing&nbsp;
up, he desperately wants to stop that from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.640
happening to anyone else and to make Gotham City&nbsp;
a better place. In the new film, fans get to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.240
Bruce devoted to finding and stopping the Riddler&nbsp;
and ridding the city of corruption. He works with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.920
James Gordon and Selina Kyle to get that job done.&nbsp;
In the end, Bruce is a hero, and it looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.280
there is still some hope for Gotham to be better.
But even as a superhero, Bruce certainly isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.160
perfect. He's human, with issues that he has&nbsp;
not fully dealt with yet, and this leads him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.440
make some bad decisions. "The Batman" highlights&nbsp;
these flaws alongside Bruce's strengths, leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.880
to one scene in the new film in which Bruce&nbsp;
arguably takes a conversation one step too far.&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.880
Chances are, you already know that the man who&nbsp;
raises him after his parents' deaths is none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.480
other than his butler, Alfred Pennyworth. Alfred&nbsp;
is a wonderful parental figure, and while Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.200
has some things he definitely needs to work&nbsp;
on, he turns out to be a pretty stand-up guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.600
But in "The Batman," the Riddler's schemes hit a&nbsp;
little too close to home for Bruce, causing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.200
to question everything he thinks he knows about&nbsp;
his father, Thomas Wayne, and in turn, Alfred.&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.680
In the first half of the film, Bruce exhibits&nbsp;
signs of carelessness when it comes to his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.480
wellbeing. Sure, he wears bulletproof armor&nbsp;
and can take care of himself in a fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.040
but he also takes unnecessary risks and&nbsp;
doesn't seem to care for much outside of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.000
"vengeance." With the new mayoral candidate, Bella&nbsp;
Reál [ray-AL], highlighting the need for change on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.280
the ground in Gotham, Alfred brings up the topic&nbsp;
of the Wayne legacy of philanthropy to Bruce.&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.720
Alfred tells Bruce that the young man&nbsp;
has no consideration for maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.200
the Wayne family legacy as upstanding&nbsp;
citizens of Gotham, and that all Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.880
cares about is bringing "justice" through Batman.
"He's not trying to inflict his kind of justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.560
He's just compelled to do it."
He doesn't even seem to give a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.360
second thought to the idea of Wayne's holdings&nbsp;
and other projects completely falling apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.000
Then when Alfred pushes once more, Bruce&nbsp;
responds emotionally, snapping back at Alfred.&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.320
While Bruce is likely pushing Alfred away to&nbsp;
keep them both from getting hurt, he does more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.720
damage than he thinks. Alfred's devotion and&nbsp;
love for the Wayne family are probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.840
basis of his identity and life. Audiences get a&nbsp;
deeper sense of this in the scene when Bruce is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.880
getting ready to attend the mayor's funeral and&nbsp;
Alfred gives Bruce his Wayne cufflinks to wear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.360
Bruce questions why Alfred has them as he's&nbsp;
technically not a Wayne. Alfred reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.760
that they were a cherished gift from Bruce's&nbsp;
father. Bruce realizes that his father giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.080
Alfred the cufflinks was a declaration&nbsp;
of Alfred becoming a part of the family.&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.720
Bruce doesn't necessarily apologize to Alfred&nbsp;
about the offense in so many words. But he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.080
realizes the truth, that Alfred really is family,&nbsp;
after the Riddler's attempt on Bruce's life almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.440
kills his devoted father figure. Bruce stays by&nbsp;
Alfred's side in the hospital as he recovers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.080
Once he wakes up, the two have a very important&nbsp;
conversation about Thomas and the things Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.200
finds out about his father from the Riddler.
"Riddler's latest. It's all about the Waynes."&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.720
Ultimately, the scene builds to them&nbsp;
having a touching moment together.&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.800
Although Bruce takes his anger and grief a&nbsp;
step too far in the earlier conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.080
he thankfully comes to his&nbsp;
senses by the end of "The Batman"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.440
and makes it clear to Alfred&nbsp;
that they are, in fact, family.&nbsp;

